## Reasoning

Use deterministic operations for exact conditions, counting, sorting, pagination, and grouping.
Use reason when the request requires interpreting meaning, intent, sentiment, quality, relevance, urgency, complaints,
or whether a record satisfies a natural-language condition.
Keyword matching may reduce a candidate set. It does not prove a subjective condition.
For a natural-language category, combine explicit structured facts with relevant narrative evidence. Prefer direct evidence, preserve
uncertainty, and return the evidence behind each classification. Do not require a canonical database marker or invent a proxy criterion.
Semantic judgment must receive all evidence required by its criteria. Keep relevant evidence lossless until that judgment is complete;
summaries, fixed excerpts, and partial histories are not substitutes for source evidence. Remove only fields known to be irrelevant.
If complete evidence does not fit a bounded reason call, follow the execution model instead of reasoning over an incomplete sample.

Inspect schema-check for the result type grammar. A batch needs an array of objects, not one object's schema for all inputs.
For arrays of strings use "string_list". (list "string") is an enum forcing the literal "string", not an array type.
Check dynamic schemas before expensive work. If the contract was wrong, correct it and rerun reasoning on the original input;
renaming invalid values cannot repair extraction. Recompute dependent summaries or report the failure.
